WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.220 --> 00:00:06.296
SPEAKER 1: Hi, and welcome
to this OpenCue tutorial.

00:00:06.296 --> 00:00:08.420
Today I'm going to walk
through submitting a render

00:00:08.420 --> 00:00:11.860
job to OpenCue and go over some
of the functionality in the Cue

00:00:11.860 --> 00:00:14.440
GUI monitoring application.

00:00:14.440 --> 00:00:16.180
Here we are inside
Maya, and we're all

00:00:16.180 --> 00:00:19.540
ready to kick off a render of
our robot dance party scene.

00:00:19.540 --> 00:00:22.060
We've set up our
Maya Render settings.

00:00:22.060 --> 00:00:24.640
This render is
going to use V-Ray.

00:00:24.640 --> 00:00:26.680
And now we'll jump
over to our render cam,

00:00:26.680 --> 00:00:28.990
just to make sure
everything looks good.

00:00:28.990 --> 00:00:30.820
Once we're ready
to submit the job,

00:00:30.820 --> 00:00:33.760
we can switch to
the OpenCue shelf

00:00:33.760 --> 00:00:37.210
and click on the
Submit or Shelf button.

00:00:37.210 --> 00:00:39.100
This will launch the
OpenCue submission form

00:00:39.100 --> 00:00:40.660
as a Maya plugin.

00:00:40.660 --> 00:00:43.840
This UI can also be launched
as a standalone application

00:00:43.840 --> 00:00:45.790
from the command line.

00:00:45.790 --> 00:00:50.760
The first thing that we're
going to do is enter a job name.

00:00:50.760 --> 00:00:55.000
And then we'll give
it a shot name.

00:00:55.000 --> 00:00:58.900
And then we'll enter a layer
name-- in this case, lighting.

00:00:58.900 --> 00:01:00.940
Then we'll select
our render cam.

00:01:00.940 --> 00:01:03.220
If we don't select one,
it'll just use the camera

00:01:03.220 --> 00:01:05.050
from our render settings.

00:01:05.050 --> 00:01:09.540
And then we'll input the
frame spec we want to render.

00:01:09.540 --> 00:01:12.020
So for more information
about this frame spec syntax,

00:01:12.020 --> 00:01:14.220
we can hit the
question mark icon.

00:01:14.220 --> 00:01:16.610
This will give some
description and examples

00:01:16.610 --> 00:01:20.230
about the frame spec syntax.

00:01:20.230 --> 00:01:22.630
Next we'll set up the job type.

00:01:22.630 --> 00:01:24.430
Job types define
what parameters are

00:01:24.430 --> 00:01:26.350
exposed in the submission UI.

00:01:26.350 --> 00:01:28.930
We'll leave it set
to Maya in this case.

00:01:28.930 --> 00:01:31.510
And then Services--
services help

00:01:31.510 --> 00:01:34.540
us define default resource
requirements and tags

00:01:34.540 --> 00:01:35.710
for this job.

00:01:35.710 --> 00:01:38.350
We'll submit this
to the Maya service,

00:01:38.350 --> 00:01:41.830
and we'll leave the min core
requirements set to zero.

00:01:41.830 --> 00:01:45.400
This allows CuBot to send
the job to any size machine.

00:01:45.400 --> 00:01:48.360
And since this is just
a single layer job,

00:01:48.360 --> 00:01:51.340
we'll leave the
dependency set to none.

00:01:51.340 --> 00:01:54.940
But perhaps you wanted
a more complex job.

00:01:54.940 --> 00:01:57.070
Maybe you wanted to
kick off a Nuke render

00:01:57.070 --> 00:01:58.480
to be dependent on this.

00:01:58.480 --> 00:02:00.955
You could hit the plus button
and configure a new Nuke

00:02:00.955 --> 00:02:06.170
layer that would be dependent
on our lighting layer.

00:02:06.170 --> 00:02:07.680
For this demo,
though, we're just

00:02:07.680 --> 00:02:11.730
going to do the simple,
single layer Maya render.

00:02:11.730 --> 00:02:14.760
And at this point we can
double check our parameters.

00:02:14.760 --> 00:02:19.330
If everything looks good, we'll
go ahead and click Submit.

00:02:19.330 --> 00:02:22.140
And we'll get a
little window pop up

00:02:22.140 --> 00:02:25.320
that says the job's been
submitted to OpenCue.

00:02:25.320 --> 00:02:28.230
And at this point, we
can jump over to Cue GUI.

00:02:31.540 --> 00:02:34.660
So there you can
see our robot's job

00:02:34.660 --> 00:02:37.580
has popped up in
the Job View there.

00:02:37.580 --> 00:02:39.520
And this is Cue GUI,
the main interface

00:02:39.520 --> 00:02:42.490
for monitoring the state
of everything in OpenCue.

00:02:42.490 --> 00:02:44.290
It's a [INAUDIBLE]
application that's built

00:02:44.290 --> 00:02:46.780
on multiple dockable
widgets, allowing the user

00:02:46.780 --> 00:02:50.020
to be able to customize the
interface to their liking.

00:02:50.020 --> 00:02:52.340
And at the top we
have the Job View,

00:02:52.340 --> 00:02:55.900
and down below is the
Monitor Host view.

00:02:55.900 --> 00:02:59.930
So we'll switch over to
the Job Details view,

00:02:59.930 --> 00:03:02.910
And then we'll double
click on our job.

00:03:02.910 --> 00:03:05.860
And this will show us
the hierarchy of the job.

00:03:05.860 --> 00:03:08.800
Going from top to bottom,
first we have a job.

00:03:08.800 --> 00:03:12.010
A job is a container
for a single submission.

00:03:12.010 --> 00:03:12.910
Then we have a layer.

00:03:12.910 --> 00:03:15.670
You can think of a
layer as a command

00:03:15.670 --> 00:03:18.370
with a frame range to run,
and at the bottom is a frame.

00:03:18.370 --> 00:03:21.340
A frame is an individual
instance of that command,

00:03:21.340 --> 00:03:25.464
with a single frame or chunk
to be executed by a host.

00:03:25.464 --> 00:03:27.130
So next we're going
to walk through some

00:03:27.130 --> 00:03:30.010
of the other
interfaces in Cue GUI.

00:03:30.010 --> 00:03:34.320
You can open up new panels
through the Views plug-in menu.

00:03:34.320 --> 00:03:37.320
We'll start with opening
the Shows interface.

00:03:37.320 --> 00:03:39.360
This view will display
a list of shows

00:03:39.360 --> 00:03:43.470
known by OpenCue and some high
level stats about that show--

00:03:43.470 --> 00:03:46.410
things like the total number
of running frames and pending

00:03:46.410 --> 00:03:48.290
frames.

00:03:48.290 --> 00:03:51.060
From there, we'll open
up the Allocations view.

00:03:51.060 --> 00:03:54.060
Allocations are a grouping
mechanism for hosts.

00:03:54.060 --> 00:03:56.640
Generally an allocation
is defined by the facility

00:03:56.640 --> 00:04:00.600
that the host lives in-- in
this case, local or cloud.

00:04:00.600 --> 00:04:02.910
And then there
will also be a tag.

00:04:02.910 --> 00:04:06.090
These tags will be applied to
any host in that allocation

00:04:06.090 --> 00:04:09.330
by default. You can
see the number of cores

00:04:09.330 --> 00:04:12.030
in each allocation here.

00:04:12.030 --> 00:04:14.940
Next we'll take a look at
the Subscriptions view.

00:04:14.940 --> 00:04:17.700
Subscriptions exist for a
show, so we'll select the show

00:04:17.700 --> 00:04:19.769
that we're interested
in, and it'll

00:04:19.769 --> 00:04:23.300
list the available
allocations for that show.

00:04:23.300 --> 00:04:27.530
This, then, defines what host
can run jobs for a given show.

00:04:27.530 --> 00:04:31.970
All the allocations show the
size, usage, and burst amount.

00:04:31.970 --> 00:04:34.100
You can edit these
parameters by right-clicking

00:04:34.100 --> 00:04:36.410
on an allocation.

00:04:36.410 --> 00:04:39.470
You can also edit the show
settings from this view

00:04:39.470 --> 00:04:42.020
by clicking on the Show
Properties button, where

00:04:42.020 --> 00:04:43.460
you can set the
core requirements

00:04:43.460 --> 00:04:47.300
and do things like enabling
or disabling booking.

00:04:47.300 --> 00:04:49.610
We'll go ahead and close
the Subscription View,

00:04:49.610 --> 00:04:52.580
and we'll walk through the
Monitor Host View next.

00:04:55.170 --> 00:04:57.270
We'll go ahead and
turn on Refresh

00:04:57.270 --> 00:05:01.080
with no filter to populate
the full list of hosts.

00:05:01.080 --> 00:05:03.150
And this will tell us
all sorts of information

00:05:03.150 --> 00:05:06.930
about the RQD hosts
active in OpenCue.

00:05:06.930 --> 00:05:08.430
We can open up the
Attributes view

00:05:08.430 --> 00:05:11.250
to explore this information
a little closer.

00:05:11.250 --> 00:05:14.100
You can see things
here like System Stats

00:05:14.100 --> 00:05:18.810
and the allocations and
tags for this host--

00:05:18.810 --> 00:05:21.900
in this case, Cloud General,
and on this other host,

00:05:21.900 --> 00:05:24.550
Local Unassigned.

00:05:24.550 --> 00:05:26.720
And if you notice, only
one of these machines

00:05:26.720 --> 00:05:27.980
is running right now.

00:05:27.980 --> 00:05:30.110
That's because our dance
party job was submitted

00:05:30.110 --> 00:05:32.100
to the local facility.

00:05:32.100 --> 00:05:34.760
So only the host with
Local General allocation

00:05:34.760 --> 00:05:37.160
is eligible to run that job.

00:05:37.160 --> 00:05:40.610
The one with Cloud
Unassigned cannot.

00:05:40.610 --> 00:05:43.230
If you need to do anything
like modify the host,

00:05:43.230 --> 00:05:46.340
you can right click on the
host and do things like change

00:05:46.340 --> 00:05:49.640
the allocation, add
tags, delete the host,

00:05:49.640 --> 00:05:54.650
lock or unlock the host,
and also leave comments.

00:05:54.650 --> 00:05:57.980
That about covers the basics
of the Host Monitor view.

00:05:57.980 --> 00:06:02.090
And next we'll look at some more
of the job configuration views.

00:06:02.090 --> 00:06:04.910
We'll start off with
the Services view.

00:06:04.910 --> 00:06:07.610
Services are a way
of classifying a job

00:06:07.610 --> 00:06:11.410
and defining some default
requirements for that job.

00:06:11.410 --> 00:06:13.390
Here we have a list
of services defined

00:06:13.390 --> 00:06:16.450
by different applications.

00:06:16.450 --> 00:06:19.540
And you can see there are
default system requirements

00:06:19.540 --> 00:06:22.840
and tags that get applied
to jobs in that service.

00:06:22.840 --> 00:06:24.610
These parameters are
defaults, though,

00:06:24.610 --> 00:06:28.000
and they can be overridden
at job submission time

00:06:28.000 --> 00:06:31.150
or once the job
has hit the queue.

00:06:31.150 --> 00:06:36.400
So we'll jump back to
the Job Details View

00:06:36.400 --> 00:06:40.910
to take a look at how
services are applied to a job.

00:06:40.910 --> 00:06:43.000
So we'll select our
job and right click

00:06:43.000 --> 00:06:44.830
on the layer we're
interested in.

00:06:44.830 --> 00:06:46.600
If we open up the
Properties dialog,

00:06:46.600 --> 00:06:49.840
we'll see something very
similar to the Services View.

00:06:49.840 --> 00:06:51.760
This information,
by default, is all

00:06:51.760 --> 00:06:54.910
populated from the service that
was selected at job submission

00:06:54.910 --> 00:06:56.050
time.

00:06:56.050 --> 00:06:58.600
Like I previously mentioned,
that's just the default,

00:06:58.600 --> 00:07:01.360
though, and it can be changed
on a layer by layer basis

00:07:01.360 --> 00:07:02.900
if needed.

00:07:02.900 --> 00:07:05.720
So we'll go ahead
and close that.

00:07:05.720 --> 00:07:09.530
And it looks like our frames
are starting to finish up.

00:07:09.530 --> 00:07:12.170
There's still a number
of frames running,

00:07:12.170 --> 00:07:14.610
but these green
ones are finished.

00:07:14.610 --> 00:07:18.452
So we'll take a look
at the Log View.

00:07:18.452 --> 00:07:20.410
If you select the log
and open up the Log View,

00:07:20.410 --> 00:07:22.750
you'll see the render
log for that frame.

00:07:22.750 --> 00:07:25.150
In this case, it's a V-Ray
log that includes all

00:07:25.150 --> 00:07:26.830
of the output from that render.

00:07:26.830 --> 00:07:28.600
There's a handy search
feature down here

00:07:28.600 --> 00:07:33.640
that allows us to quickly search
for specific text in the log.

00:07:33.640 --> 00:07:38.170
You can also open up the log by
double clicking on the frame.

00:07:38.170 --> 00:07:41.710
In this case, it will open
in [? MacVim ?] for me.

00:07:41.710 --> 00:07:46.810
You can edit this by specifying
your editor in an environment

00:07:46.810 --> 00:07:49.090
variable.

00:07:49.090 --> 00:07:53.590
That about covers the basics
of the OpenCue GUI interface.

00:07:53.590 --> 00:07:55.500
Thanks for watching.

